Title: Judge's Crossword Puzzle No. 84
Author: Catherine MacMillan
Date: 1928-12-29
Byline: Submitted by Catherine MacMillan, Chicago, Ill.
Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1928-12-29_95/page/n31/mode/1up
Puzzle-Number: 84
Solution-Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1929-01-05_96/page/n32/mode/1up


STOOLS###BATONS
TURNIPS#HORIZON
AT#EARL#ONCE#RI
R###RAIL#EH###V
CHRISTMAS#IMAGE
HEAT###MT#TIDAL
#MY#SAMBO#ENDS#
###WISECRACK###
#AGOG#CHEST#HA#
SPURN#CO###PICA
HENNA#APPRAISED
E###TO#SAIL###D
LO#TUNA#SOLO#HE
LABORED#STOPPER
STONES###SWEARS


A1. These have no backing and are always being sat upon. ~ STOOLS
A7. These help make a good time. ~ BATONS
A13. The roots of these plants are edible. ~ TURNIPS
A15. The higher you go, the farther away this goes. ~ HORIZON
A16. Near. ~ AT
A17. Here is a noble man. ~ EARL
A18. This is always starting stories. ~ ONCE
A19. Providence supplies the capital for this. (Abbr.) ~ RI
A20. This is always getting run over by a railroad train. ~ RAIL
A22. An expression of inquiry. ~ EH
A23. This will even make a Scotchman give. ~ CHRISTMAS
A28. This is often thrown on the screen. ~ IMAGE
A32. This will give you a hot time. ~ HEAT
A33. Something hard to get over. (Abbr.) ~ MT
A34. This is one kind of a permanent wave. ~ TIDAL
A35. This is personal. ~ MY
A36. This fellow sports a real coon-skin. (Proper name.) ~ SAMBO
A39. It is often hard to make these meet. ~ ENDS
A40. Some people think this is a joke. ~ WISECRACK
A42. In a state of excitement. ~ AGOG
A44. This is often filled with hope. ~ CHEST
A45. In order to get a laugh out of this you will have to repeat it. ~ HA
A47. A contemptuous thing to do. ~ SPURN
A48. This stands for company. (That is more than some servants will do.) ~ CO
A49. This is an average type. ~ PICA
A51. This makes a colorful blonde. ~ HENNA
A52. You should have this done to a second-hand car before you buy it. ~ APPRAISED
A56. Toward. ~ TO
A58. A wind instrument. ~ SAIL
A59. Behold. ~ LO
A61. This poor fish is always getting canned. ~ TUNA
A63. This would never do for a chorus. ~ SOLO
A65. This is quite masculine. ~ HE
A66. Anyone who has done this knows what work is. ~ LABORED
A68. This is something that comes out of a flask. ~ STOPPER
A70. The biggest pebbles on the beach. ~ STONES
A71. What a man does when he changes a tire. ~ SWEARS

D1. This makes the laundry rather stiff. (Wait till you get the bill.) ~ STARCH
D2. An ancient king—this is now a dry subject. ~ TUT
D3. A conjunction. ~ OR
D4. This is on the watch after twelve. ~ ONE
D5. These can't get down to the truth of the matter. ~ LIARS
D6. A fabled character who was partial to meat that was lean. ~ SPRAT
D7. This is going to the dogs. ~ BONE
D8. A man who once had designs on the White House. ~ ARCHITECT
D9. Those who dislike holding the strings might do this. ~ TIE
D10. The land of an old wizard. ~ OZ
D11. A negative connective. ~ NOR
D12. This is often heard in Al Jolson's audiences. ~ SNIVEL
D14. This is considered good form for a modern girl. ~ SLIM
D15. An interjection. ~ HO
D21. What Mary's little pet became. ~ LAMBCHOPS
D24. This used to cover the insteps and now it barely covers the step-ins. ~ HEM
D25. A line from "Old Man Sunshine." ~ RAY
D26. A pronoun. ~ IT
D27. Here is where you get something for your money. ~ STORE
D29. This has trimmed many a rich person. ~ MINK
D30. What you will have to do if you get a high bridge score. ~ ADD
D31. This makes the taxis go up. ~ GAS
D36. The value of this depends on the owner. ~ SIGNATURE
D37. In like manner. ~ AS
D38. The birthplace of Mohammed. ~ MECCA
D40. What was done with Al Smith's derby. ~ WORN
D41. Like. ~ AS
D42. This makes a lot of monkey-business. ~ APE
D43. This makes a daily report in Chicago. ~ GUN
D45. Masculine possessive pronoun. ~ HIS
D46. This is even greater than a king. ~ ACE
D47. These are always lying around on the seashore. ~ SHELLS
D49. A mixture of types. ~ PI
D50. These make very good summers. ~ ADDERS
D53. What people do to get by. ~ PASS
D54. You will sometimes find these at bargain counters. ~ RIOTS
D55. Parents didn't used to do this. ~ ALLOW
D57. Units. ~ ONES
D60. The kind of a meal a Scotchman likes. ~ OAT
D61. A heavyweight. ~ TON
D62. You will find this in almost any magazine. (Abbr.) ~ AD
D64. This is open for a good poet. ~ OPE
D65. This is the female for him. ~ HER
D67. A tramp in the woods. ~ BO
D69. The head of a filling station. ~ PA


